cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "vcibayes: layered Bayesian networks for cohort studies"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Overmars
    given-names: Malin
    name-suffix: PhD
    affiliation: "UMC Utrecht, Vascular Cognitive Impairment research group"
repository-code: "https://github.com/umcu/vci-bayes"
license: MIT
version: 1.2.0
date-released: "2026-08-12"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.17302710
    description: "Concept DOI for all versions"
abstract: >-
  vcibayes learns Bayesian networks from cohort data under a structure
  specified by the investigator. Variables are grouped into ordered layers,
  and the learner may only draw an arc from a layer to itself or to a layer
  further down that order, so the recovered structure respects the assumed
  direction of influence. Dropout is represented as a selection layer
  downstream of the outcomes, allowing non-random loss to follow-up to be
  modelled rather than imputed away. Beyond the layer ordering, individual
  arcs may be forbidden or required, and variables may be pinned as roots or
  sinks; such constraints may only narrow what the ordering permits, so the
  ordering remains a complete statement of the assumed structure. The
  analysis is declared in a YAML specification rather than in code, covering
  the layers and their order, the arc constraints, network variants,
  discretisation, random seed, bootstrap counts, participant profiles and
  sensitivity sweeps, so that it can be published alongside a manuscript. The package also provides bootstrap estimates of arc stability,
  mutual-information rankings of variables against each outcome, and
  single-variable sensitivity analysis with a check for conditioning on
  mediators.
keywords:
  - Bayesian network
  - structure learning
  - cohort study
  - vascular cognitive impairment
  - pyAgrum
